  "text": "We are given:\n\n- A **regular $2n$-gon** inscribed in a circle of radius $ r $.\n- A **rhombus $ R_n $** is formed by connecting the **midpoints of the sides** of this polygon.\n- $ K_n $ is the **area of the region inside the circle and outside the rhombus** $ R_n $.\n- We are to **find the smallest $ n $** such that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{K_{n+1}}{K_n} < 1.01\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Area of the Regular $2n$-gon\n\nThe area $ A_{2n} $ of a regular $2n$-gon with radius $ r $ is given by:\n\n$$\nA_{2n} = 2n r^2 \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2n}\\right)\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Area of the Rhombus $ R_n $\n\nEach rhombus is formed by connecting the midpoints of the sides of the $2n$-gon. Since the $2n$-gon is regular and centered at the origin, its midpoints will lie on a circle of radius $ r/2 $.\n\nTo find the area of the rhombus $ R_n $, we can note that:\n\n- The rhombus is formed by connecting the midpoints of the sides of the polygon.\n- The rhombus is **equiangular** (all internal angles equal), and its **diagonals** are the **same length** as the distance between midpoints.\n\nThis rhombus is also called the **midpoint rhombus** of the $2n$-gon.\n\nThe area of the rhombus $ R_n $ can be calculated as:\n\n$$\nA_{R_n} = A_{2n} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3n}\n$$\n\nThis is because the rhombus is **3/4 of the area of the original polygon**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Area of the Region Inside the Circle and Outside the Rhombus\n\nThe area $ K_n $ is:\n\n$$\nK_n = A_{\\text{circle}} - A_{R_n}\n$$\n\nWe know:\n\n- The area of the circle is $ \\pi r^2 $\n- The area of the rhombus is $ \\frac{2}{3n} A_{2n} = \\frac{2}{3n} \\cdot 2n r^2 \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2n}\\right) = \\frac{4 r^2}{3} \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2n}\\right) $\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nK_n = \\pi r^2 - \\frac{4 r^2}{3} \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2n}\\right)\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Ratio of Areas $ \\frac{K_{n+1}}{K_n} $\n\nLet’s compute $ K_{n+1} $:\n\n- The area of the next rhombus $ R_{n+1} $ will be $ \\frac{2}{3(n+1)} A_{2(n+1)} = \\frac{2}{3(n+1)} \\cdot 2(n+1) r^2 \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2(n+1)}\\right) = \\frac{4 r^2}{3} \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2(n+1)}\\right) $\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nK_{n+1} = \\pi r^2 - \\frac{4 r^2}{3} \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2(n+1)}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, compute the ratio:\n\n$$\n\\frac{K_{n+1}}{K_n} = \\frac{\\pi r^2 - \\frac{4 r^2}{3} \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2(n+1)}\\right)}{\\pi r^2 - \\frac{4 r^2}{3} \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2n}\\right)}\n$$\n\nWe want:\n\n$$\n\\frac{K_{n+1}}{K_n} < 1.01\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Approximate the Ratio\n\nLet’s approximate $ \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2n}\\right) $ using the Taylor expansion:\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2n}\\right) \\approx \\frac{\\pi}{2n} - \\frac{\\pi^3}{48 n^3}\n$$\n\nThen:\n\n$$\n\\frac{K_{n+1}}{K_n} \\approx \\frac{1 - \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{\\pi}{2n}}{1 - \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{\\pi}{2n}} = 1\n$$\n\nSo the ratio is approximately **close to 1**, and we are told that the **ratio must be less than 1.01**.\n\nTo find the smallest $ n $ such that $ \\frac{K_{n+1}}{K_n} < 1.01 $, we can set:\n\n$$\n\\frac{K_{n+1}}{K_n} \\approx 1 + \\left( \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{\\pi}{2n} \\right)^2 < 1.01\n$$\n\n$$\n\\left( \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{\\pi}{2n} \\right)^2 < 0.01\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{\\pi}{2n} < \\sqrt{0.01} = 0.1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi}{3n} < 0.1\n$$\n\n$$\nn > \\frac{2\\pi}{3 \\cdot 0.1} = \\frac{2\\pi}{0.3} = \\frac{20\\pi}{3} \\approx 20.94\n$$\n\nSo the smallest integer $ n $ satisfying this is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{21}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{21}\n$$",
